---
id: retire-ix-git-hooks
postedAt: "2026-07-31T00:45:00-07:00"
title: "ix drops its git hooks; CI already ran every check they did"
links:
  - label: "the removal"
    href: "https://github.com/indexable-inc/ix/pull/9311"
tags:
  - ci
  - workflow
---

`ix` carried three hooks under `.githooks/`, installed by entering the
devshell: a `pre-commit` running astlog over the staged `.nix` files, a
`pre-push` running the stale-base check and a `treefmt` gate, and a guard
whose only job was refusing a hook that had come from a different worktree
of the same clone. All three are gone, and no check went with them.

Each one already had a wider CI gate. The whole-tree `astlog-check` and
`treefmtCheck` stages of `ci-lint-checks` cover both lint hooks and cover
more than the staged subset ever did; `check-merge-integrity.sh` runs from
`run-ci-phases.sh` with the same arguments. What the hooks added was not
coverage but earliness, and the price was a devshell shell hook, a configure
script, a fixture suite, a lint stage, and an eval-time assert keeping a
count printed inside `pre-commit` in sync with the real stage list.

Neither repo now runs any hook. `nix run .#lint` in index and `just lint`
in ix are the local gates, and running one is on you: a clean commit says
nothing about lint. `pre-commit install` refuses in both trees, and writing
a config to make it work is how ix ended up with a rule that had never run
on a single commit in its life.

One thing this does not fix. The stale-base check was put in `pre-push`
because a merge can outrun its own required checks, which is what happened
twice on 2026-07-27: both merges came from a ruleset bypass actor seconds
after the run started, and the merge cancelled the run. A hook was never a
control on that, since the same bypass skips it, but nothing local catches
a stale-base tree before a push now either. The real fix is a ruleset
without bypass actors, tracked in ENG-11624.
